      "writtenOn": "2020-02-14T03:58:30Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Why this difference ? Why can\u0027t Settings store its profile the same way an app does ?",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 402,
        "startChar": 40,
        "endLine": 403,
        "endChar": 77
      },
      "revId": "c38c23ec319497ed8a772f7f360d26a8938ff26f",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "a58187ae_1779eb46",
        "filename": "core/java/android/net/Ikev2VpnProfile.java",
        "patchSetId": 44
      },
      "lineNbr": 403,
      "author": {
        "id": 1221725
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-02-14T07:51:11Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The big difference is in the format that Settings accepts parameters in; for PSKs, Settings accepts Strings, while the VpnManager accepts the more flexible byte[]\n\nSimilarly, Settings accepts certificate/private-key names, rather than the full certificate the VpnManager accepts.\n\nSo in order to build an Ikev2VpnProfile from Settings, we need to special-case the extraction of the private key from the keystore, and treat the VpnProfile\u0027s String as a String, rather than a base64-encoded byte[].\n\nThat said, it IS possible to encode the PSK specially using encodeForIpsecSecret() when storing the profile, but that just shards the special casing into the Settings app.",
      "parentUuid": "524a8faf_beb0e13f",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 402,
        "startChar": 40,
        "endLine": 403,
        "endChar": 77
      },
      "revId": "c38c23ec319497ed8a772f7f360d26a8938ff26f",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "e09abd80_619d7408",
        "filename": "core/java/android/net/Ikev2VpnProfile.java",
        "patchSetId": 44
      },
      "lineNbr": 403,
      "author": {
        "id": 1015428
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-02-14T08:11:16Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Shouldn\u0027t Settings convert the string to a byte[] ?\n\nI don\u0027t understand the special case argument – this here is framework code, it relies on the Vpn profile which is framework code, and has a contractual format. Saying Settings uses a different format and testing for it here is special-casing ; having Settings do the same thing as everyone else seems like the opposite of special-casing.",
      "parentUuid": "a58187ae_1779eb46",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 402,
        "startChar": 40,
        "endLine": 403,
        "endChar": 77
      },
      "revId": "c38c23ec319497ed8a772f7f360d26a8938ff26f",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "b5af85e0_22fa95d8",
        "filename": "core/java/android/net/Ikev2VpnProfile.java",
        "patchSetId": 44
      },
      "lineNbr": 403,
      "author": {
        "id": 1221725
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-02-18T04:39:43Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Semi-done; Since we already pull the certificates out of the keystore in the startLegacyVpn(), so I changed it to format the IPsec secret properly there (and removed it from here)\n\nIt\u0027s not quite pulled out into the Settings app, but since we\u0027re already having to do some adapting in startLegacyVpn() to use the parsed certificates, it seemed to make sense to add it there.\n\nAlso, since the USER_PRIVATE_KEY prefix is added in the startLegacyVpn(), it felt like the better place to add the KEYSTORE_ALIAS_PREFIX as well so that the KEYSTORE_ALIAS_PREFIX is seen, and stripped first.\n\nThoughts?",
      "parentUuid": "e09abd80_619d7408",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 402,
        "startChar": 40,
        "endLine": 403,
        "endChar": 77
      },
      "revId": "c38c23ec319497ed8a772f7f360d26a8938ff26f",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "f3d1017f_40ff8bf5",
        "filename": "services/core/java/com/android/server/connectivity/Vpn.java",
